{"type":"message","id":"0a08aa96","parentId":"df3f967b","timestamp":"2026-07-28T12:16:27.909Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-88e59b47a84b69e6","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 3 block(s) in /tmp/coinography-run-20260728-120854-96194-23933/article/raw.md."}],"details":{"diff":"    ...\n 17 The Thai SEC's complaint centers on events that began in May 2021, when hackers stole customer assets across 16 cryptocurrencies from the exchange. Rather than reporting the incident, Bitkub allegedly filed daily Form DA 1 reports from May 10 through October 30, 2021, showing no material change in its asset balance despite the massive theft.\n 18 \n 19 ### False Filings Masked The Theft\n 20 \n-21 According to the regulator, Bitkub's filings contained false information and gave the impression that customer assets were being held normally. The SEC stated that Bitkub did not report in a manner consistent with the theft incident. Named in the complaint are Sakolkorn Sakavee and Thaweesap Rawan, who were Bitkub directors at the time and responsible for submitting the reports.\n+21 According to the regulator, Bitkub's filings contained false information and gave the impression that customer assets were being held normally. The SEC stated that Bitkub did not report in a manner consistent with the theft incident. Named in the complaint are Sakolkorn Sakavee and Thaweesap Rawan, who were Bitkub directors at the time and responsible for submitting the reports. The regulator alleges that both individuals made false entries in company documents during the period when the theft remained concealed.\n 22 \n 23 ### Co-Founders Silently Replaced Assets\n 24 \n 25 The SEC found that Bitkub began procuring replacement digital assets on October 31, 2021. [Bitcoin Foundation](https://bitcoinfoundation.org/news/crypto-companies-news/thai-sec-accuses-bitkub-of-concealing-51-million-hack/) reported that the exchange's co-founders absorbed the loss by buying the same cryptocurrencies in the same quantities, although the stolen assets were never recovered. Bitkub claims neither the company nor its customers suffered losses.\n    ...\n 45 The allegations remain unadjudicated. The criminal action process in Thailand requires an investigation before prosecutors decide whether to bring the case. No timeline has been announced for when the inquiry might conclude.\n 46 \n 47 ### Market Impact And Trader Takeaways\n 48 \n-49 For crypto traders, the Bitkub case highlights the risks of exchange opacity. Even when customer balances are eventually made whole, regulatory filings may not reflect exchange vulnerabilities in real time. The five-month gap between the hack and the start of asset replacement demonstrates how exchanges can operate without full transparency during crisis periods.\n+49 For crypto traders, the Bitkub case highlights the risks of exchange opacity. Even when customer balances are eventually made whole, regulatory filings may not reflect exchange vulnerabilities in real time. The five-month gap between the hack and the start of asset replacement demonstrates how exchanges can operate without full transparency during crisis periods. This case adds to growing regulatory scrutiny of crypto exchanges in Asian markets.\n 50 \n 51 ## Conclusion\n 52 \n 53 The Thai SEC's criminal complaint against Bitkub for concealing a $53 million theft underscores how exchanges can obscure major security breaches from regulators and customers. While Bitkub argues the nondisclosure prevented a bank run, the five-month gap between the hack and asset replacement raises serious transparency concerns. Traders should monitor developments closely as the investigation proceeds.\n    ...\n 81 **Sources:**\n 82 - CryptoSlate: https://cryptoslate.com/how-a-crypto-exchange-secretly-hid-53m-in-stolen-crypto-to-prevent-a-bank-run-and-why-the-sec-is-finally-coming-for-its-directors/\n 83 - Bitcoin Foundation: https://bitcoinfoundation.org/news/crypto-companies-news/thai-sec-accuses-bitkub-of-concealing-51-million-hack/\n 84 \n-85 [Word Count: 1102]\n+85 [Word Count: 1112]","firstChangedLine":21},"isError":false,"timestamp":1785240987909}}
