{"type":"message","id":"913a7c92","parentId":"c442e1f0","timestamp":"2026-07-28T12:17:56.847Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-9003ca311b5171c7","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"total 8\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 bhard bhard 4096 Jul 28 12:16 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 9 bhard bhard 4096 Jul 28 12:17 ..\n-rw------- 1 bhard bhard    0 Jul 28 12:17 final.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 bhard bhard    0 Jul 28 12:17 raw.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 bhard bhard    0 Jul 28 12:17 with-image.md"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":173,"aggregated":"total 8\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 bhard bhard 4096 Jul 28 12:16 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 9 bhard bhard 4096 Jul 28 12:17 ..\n-rw------- 1 bhard bhard    0 Jul 28 12:17 final.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 bhard bhard    0 Jul 28 12:17 raw.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 bhard bhard    0 Jul 28 12:17 with-image.md","cwd":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-orchestrator"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1785241076847}}
{"type":"message","id":"223a0f7c","parentId":"913a7c92","timestamp":"2026-07-28T12:18:15.066Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9549001176095e18","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/coinography-run-20260728-120854-96194-23933/article/raw.md","content":"META\n- SEO Title: Bitkub $53M Theft: Thai SEC Files Criminal Complaint\n- Meta Description: Thai SEC files criminal complaint over Bitkub $53M theft concealed in daily reports, alleging false filings after a 2021 hack.\n- URL Slug: bitkub-53m-theft-thai-sec\n- Categories: Exploits News, SEC\n- Primary Keyword: Bitkub $53M theft\n- Secondary Keywords: Thai SEC, crypto exchange hack, Bitkub concealed theft, bank run prevention\n\n---\n\n# Bitkub $53M Theft: Exchange Hid Hack From Thai SEC\n\nThailand's Securities and Exchange Commission has filed a criminal complaint over a Bitkub $53M theft that the country's largest cryptocurrency exchange allegedly concealed from regulators for nearly six months. The July 23, 2026 complaint alleges that Bitkub submitted false daily net-capital reports that failed to reflect the impact of a May 2021 cyberattack, while two former directors oversaw the cover-up.\n\n## The $53 Million Hack And Cover-up\n\nThe Thai SEC's complaint centers on events that began in May 2021, when hackers stole customer assets across 16 cryptocurrencies from the exchange. Rather than reporting the incident, Bitkub allegedly filed daily Form DA 1 reports from May 10 through October 30, 2021, showing no material change in its asset balance despite the massive theft.\n\n### False Filings Masked The Theft\n\nAccording to the regulator, Bitkub's filings contained false information and gave the impression that customer assets were being held normally. The SEC stated that [Bitkub](https://cryptoslate.com/how-a-crypto-exchange-secretly-hid-53m-in-stolen-crypto-to-prevent-a-bank-run-and-why-the-sec-is-finally-coming-for-its-directors/) did not report in a manner consistent with the theft incident. Named in the complaint are Sakolkorn Sakavee and Thaweesap Rawan, who were Bitkub directors at the time and responsible for submitting the reports. The regulator alleges that both individuals made false entries in company documents during the period when the theft remained concealed.\n\n### Co-Founders Silently Replaced Assets\n\nThe SEC found that Bitkub began procuring replacement digital assets on October 31, 2021. [Bitcoin Foundation](https://bitcoinfoundation.org/news/crypto-companies-news/thai-sec-accuses-bitkub-of-concealing-51-million-hack/) reported that the exchange's co-founders absorbed the loss by buying the same cryptocurrencies in the same quantities, although the stolen assets were never recovered. Bitkub claims neither the company nor its customers suffered losses.\n\n## Bank Run Prevention Or Regulatory Evasion?\n\nBitkub acknowledged that personnel responsible for disclosures chose not to reveal that assets from one company wallet had been stolen. The company said the decision was intended to prevent a bank run while it worked to replace the assets.\n\n### Exchange Defends Its Actions\n\nIn its response to the complaint, Bitkub stated that had a bank run occurred, customers could have suffered widespread losses and Thailand's digital-asset industry could have been harmed. The exchange disputed that the accused individuals acted fraudulently, insisting the incident did not result from fraudulent conduct.\n\n### Unresolved Questions On Timing\n\nThe SEC noted in a separate September 2025 update that customer digital assets held by Bitkub were safe and complete as of September 8, 2025. However, that finding covered a later point in time and did not resolve the regulator's allegations about what Bitkub reported in 2021. Asset replacement and regulatory reporting remain separate questions in the complaint, making the timeline critical for understanding the full scope of the alleged concealment.\n\n## What Happens Next For Bitkub\n\nThe complaint now moves to Thailand's Economic Crime Suppression Division for investigation. An inquiry official will examine the case and decide whether to recommend prosecution. If forwarded to a public prosecutor and ultimately a court, Bitkub and its former directors could face formal charges.\n\n### Uncertain Timeline For Resolution\n\nThe allegations remain unadjudicated. The criminal action process in Thailand requires an investigation before prosecutors decide whether to bring the case. No timeline has been announced for when the inquiry might conclude.\n\n### Market Impact And Trader Takeaways\n\nFor crypto traders, the Bitkub $53M theft case highlights the risks of exchange opacity. Even when customer balances are eventually made whole, regulatory filings may not reflect exchange vulnerabilities in real time. The five-month gap between the hack and the start of asset replacement demonstrates how exchanges can operate without full transparency during crisis periods. This case adds to growing regulatory scrutiny of crypto exchanges in Asian markets.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe Thai SEC's criminal complaint against Bitkub for concealing a $53 million theft underscores how exchanges can obscure major security breaches from regulators and customers. While Bitkub argues the nondisclosure prevented a bank run, the five-month gap between the hack and asset replacement raises serious transparency concerns. Traders should monitor developments closely as the investigation proceeds, as the outcome could set precedents for exchange disclosure requirements across Southeast Asia.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. What did Bitkub allegedly hide from regulators?**\n\nBitkub allegedly hid a $53 million theft from May 2021 by filing daily reports that failed to show the cyberattack's impact on its asset balance for nearly six months.\n\n**2. Who is named in the Thai SEC complaint?**\n\nThe complaint names Bitkub Online and two former directors, Sakolkorn Sakavee and Thaweesap Rawan, who were responsible for submitting the required Form DA 1 reports.\n\n**3. How did Bitkub explain the concealment?**\n\nBitkub said the decision not to disclose the theft was intended to prevent a bank run, and that co-founders replaced the stolen assets without customers suffering losses.\n\n**4. What happens next in the case?**\n\nAn inquiry official will investigate before deciding whether to recommend prosecution. A public prosecutor would then review the case, and a court would adjudicate only if charges are brought.\n\n**5. Were customer funds ultimately safe?**\n\nBitkub claims co-founders replaced all stolen assets and that the SEC confirmed in September 2025 that customer holdings were complete, though that finding addressed a later timeframe.\n\n**6. What cryptocurrencies were stolen in the attack?**\n\nAccording to the SEC, hackers stole customer assets across 16 different cryptocurrencies in the May 2021 cyberattack, with the total value reaching approximately 1.7 billion baht or roughly $53 million at the time.\n\n**Sources:**\n- CryptoSlate: https://cryptoslate.com/how-a-crypto-exchange-secretly-hid-53m-in-stolen-crypto-to-prevent-a-bank-run-and-why-the-sec-is-finally-coming-for-its-directors/\n- Bitcoin Foundation: https://bitcoinfoundation.org/news/crypto-companies-news/thai-sec-accuses-bitkub-of-concealing-51-million-hack/\n\n[Word Count: 1113]\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"bedrock-mantle","model":"zai.glm-5","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1785241076851,"responseId":"chatcmpl-18b7f278-f5ac-4026-9417-e462b4b947da"}}
